From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Norman Herms <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 16:33:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] phy: allwinner: a733-combophy: Add DisplayPort

The first combo PHY of the A733 carries USB3 or the four main link lanes
of the DisplayPort transmitter. The Type-C port controller asks for the
lanes through the DisplayPort alternate mode, so the PHY is the mode
switch of the connector.

Register a Type-C mode switch and a DisplayPort PHY. Pin assignments C
and E hand all four lanes to DisplayPort: USB3 is held in reset, the
board's lane polarity and the lane map are set, and the link is released
with the reset sequence of the vendor code (user manual sections
18.2.6.6 to 18.2.6.10, 18.2.6.25 and 18.2.6.28). Any other state gives
the lanes back to USB3. The transmitter sets lane count, rate, swing
and pre-emphasis through phy_configure(), in the order and with the
checks of the Cadence Torrent driver, whose PMA and swing sequence
this is.

The USB3 controller has no SuperSpeed lanes then, and it leaves its core
soft reset only with a PIPE clock. Without one, a controller that probes
after the lanes went to DisplayPort fails with -ETIMEDOUT, is not probed
again and takes USB 2.0 of the port with it. Seen on the Radxa Cubie A7S
with a DisplayPort monitor at the Type-C port at power-on: "DWC3
controller soft reset failed", probe error -110. The manual lets the CCU
feed the PIPE clock of the controller (18.2.6.2; USB2_U2_PIPE is its
clock source in 18.4.3, register in 4.1.6.204), so the PHY enables the
select of the SERDES CCU before it stops USB3 and releases it when the
lanes go back to USB3, after USB3 was started again if the controller
holds the PHY. The clock is optional and its rate is not set here: the
device tree gives it.

Differences from the vendor code: the plug direction is written while
the PHY is in reset; the USB3 lane polarity is set at every USB3 start,
and USB3 is started again when the lanes return; the PHY leaves IDDQ
before the first PMA access also when DisplayPort comes back after an
unplug, as a PMA access in IDDQ stalls the bus; swing and pre-emphasis
are checked against DisplayPort's limits; the lane map is the identity
in both plug directions.

This patch sets up 2.7 Gb/s only. The reset steps, handshake timeouts
and most swing settings are vendor values, not measured on our boards,
and neither is the PIPE clock switch with a USB3 device in use.

A rate change that fails to release the reset leaves no rate set, so no
swing request is taken until the next one succeeds. An unbind in
DisplayPort mode puts the link in reset and releases the clocks.

Not covered: pin assignments D and F, spread spectrum clocking.

Tested on Radxa Cubie A7S: booted three times on one board with a
DisplayPort monitor plugged in before power-on; each time the combo PHY
was in DisplayPort mode before the USB3 controller probed, the
controller left its core soft reset and enumerated the billboard device
of the adapter, and the sink computed the CRC of a white picture from
before this change.

Assisted-by: LLM
